## Topics

BOM and should cost, supplier discovery and vetting, MOQ and price breaks, incoterms
and landed cost, negotiation levers, quality control and inspection, dual sourcing,
sampling protocol.

## Look for

Cost structures by component type. Volume break points. Negotiation levers and what
each is worth. Incoterm implications on landed cost. Quality control steps and their
cost. Customs and import data as a supplier research method, which is public,
structured and the strongest lever in the room.

## Skip

Negotiation advice with no benchmark behind the ask. **Sourcing-agency business
content**: how to start a sourcing company and win B2B clients is a different business.
Keep only the factory negotiation and specification material from such sources.

## The gap in this folder

Sourcing and factory relationships are covered. **BOM and should-cost material is
not**, and that is the core of the module: breaking a product into components and
knowing what each line should cost. Prioritise it.

## The failure mode

Cutting cost and raising returns. The savings appear immediately and the returns
appear a quarter later. Every cost cut needs a predicted return-rate risk attached.

## Scope mix

Mostly growth. Baseline: every supplier change is gated on a sample approval and a 90
day return rate watch.

## Done when

You can build a BOM with a target price per line and defend every target with a
source.

